# Development Workflow & Best Practices

## Project Setup
- Use Android Studio for development
- Sync Gradle files after any dependency changes
- Run on API level 24+ (Android 7.0+)
- Use Kotlin 1.9.10 and Compose BOM 2023.10.01

## Building & Running
- Debug build: `./gradlew assembleDebug`
- Run on device: `./gradlew installDebug`
- Clean build: `./gradlew clean`

## Code Organization
- Follow MVVM architecture strictly
- Keep UI components in `ui/` package
- Keep data layer in `data/` package
- Use descriptive package names

## Dependencies
- Core dependencies in [app/build.gradle.kts](mdc:app/build.gradle.kts)
- Compose BOM for version management
- Retrofit for networking (configured for future use)
- Coil for image loading
- Kotlin Serialization for JSON handling

## Testing
- Unit tests for ViewModels and Repository
- UI tests for Compose components
- Integration tests for data flow
- Mock data for consistent testing

## Git Workflow
- Use feature branches for new development
- Commit messages should be descriptive
- Include `.gitignore` for build artifacts
- Document major changes in README

## Performance
- Use `remember` and `derivedStateOf` for expensive computations
- Implement proper loading states
- Use `LazyColumn` and `LazyRow` for large lists
- Optimize image loading with Coil

## Debugging
- Use Android Studio's Layout Inspector for Compose
- Log important events and errors
- Use breakpoints for debugging ViewModels
- Monitor memory usage and performance

## Documentation
- Keep README.md updated
- Document complex UI components
- Add comments for business logic
- Maintain API documentation
